Interview Question
What is kube-proxy?

kube-proxy runs on every Kubernetes node and watches Services and Endpoints. It programs networking rules so traffic sent to a Service IP can be forwarded to the correct backend pods.

What is iptables?

iptables is a Linux packet filtering and NAT framework. kube-proxy traditionally uses iptables rules to implement Kubernetes Service load balancing through destination NAT.

What is eBPF?

eBPF is a Linux kernel technology that allows programs to run inside the kernel. Solutions like Cilium use eBPF to perform Service load balancing, network policy enforcement, and observability without relying on kube-proxy iptables rules.

One-Line Difference
Plain Text
kube-proxy = Creates networking rules
iptables = Traditional mechanism used by kube-proxy
eBPF = Modern kernel-based alternative used by Cilium
Easy Memory Trick
Plain Text
Old Kubernetes:
Pod -> kube-proxy -> iptables -> Pod
 
Modern Cilium:
Pod -> eBPF -> Pod

Q: What is Identity-Based Security in Cilium?

Answer:

Cilium uses security identities derived from Kubernetes labels instead of IP addresses. Each workload receives a unique identity, and eBPF enforces security policies based on these identities. This makes policies independent of pod IP changes and provides scalable, Kubernetes-native security.

One-Line Interview Summary

Traditional firewalls secure IP-to-IP communication, whereas Cilium secures workload-to-workload communication using label-based identities enforced directly in the kernel through eBPF.

